# 30. 🧠 Sabse simple architecture

Tumhari ecommerce website ko roughly aise yaad rakho:

```text
             ┌──────────────┐
             │   CUSTOMER   │
             └──────┬───────┘
                    │
                    ↓
             ┌──────────────┐
             │   FRONTEND   │
             └──────┬───────┘
                    │ API
                    ↓
             ┌──────────────┐
             │   BACKEND    │
             └──────┬───────┘
                    │
                    ↓
             ┌──────────────┐
             │   DATABASE   │
             └──────────────┘
                    ↑
                    │
             ┌──────┴───────┐
             │    ADMIN     │
             │    PANEL     │
             └──────────────┘
```

Aur multi-vendor hone ki wajah se:

```text
Customer ──────┐
               │
Seller ────────┼──→ Backend ─→ Database
               │
Admin ─────────┘
```

### Short me har layer:

**Frontend** → user ko UI dikhata hai.

**Backend** → business logic aur API handle karta hai.

**API** → frontend/backend ke beech communication ka interface.

**Database** → data permanently store karta hai.

**Admin Panel** → platform control karta hai.

**Seller Panel** → seller apna business manage karta hai.

**Customer Panel** → customer shopping/order tracking karta hai.

**JWT** → authenticated user ka token.

**Authentication** → user ki identity verify karna.

**Authorization** → user ko kya-kya karne ki permission hai, ye decide karna.

**KPI** → important performance measurement.

**AOV** → average order ki value.

**Audit Log** → actions ki history.

**Moderation** → content/reviews ko check aur control karna.

**Approval Queue** → approval ka wait kar rahe products ki list.

**API Endpoint** → backend ka specific URL/function jahan frontend request bhejta hai, jaise `/api/order/list`.

Isliye tumhare Admin Panel ko simple language me **"ecommerce website ka command center"** bol sakte ho.





